the harlem cultural festival felt like  lightning in a bottle the harlem  cultural festival of 1969 welcome to the  holland culture festival nobody ever  heard of the harlem culture festival  nobody would believe that happened 50  000 beautiful people here in mountain  park my producers robert five joseph  patel and david dinnerstein they showed  me that footage  this was real  a day after the death of dr martin  luther king after the assassination of  martin luther king many in inner city  from detroit to philadelphia to atlanta  places were just erupting and anger  righteous anger people for that year had  been dealing with constant loss there's  so many people who need help the loss of  the kennedys the loss of malcolm x  martin luther king all the civil rights  leaders  not wanting to repeat what happened the  summer of 68 the city allowed for this  festival to happen we needed something  to really reach out and touch us we  needed that music  [Music]  growing up in a musical family my dad  would often play the apollo theater in  harlem during that period amir is  someone who knows a lot about music and  it's very authentic i'm the guy that  watches every music documentary and i'm  always like they got that wrong like  that's the wrong year he can go down a  really nerdy path right like this bass  player used to play with that guitarist  and they used to have a band before this  and they did this one song that was a  cover of another song even people with  the highest musical intelligence had no  idea that this took place like wait a  minute like stevie wonder and the  staples singers and sly on the family  stone that happened in 69 and no one  ever heard of this i was like okay well  i guess this is my destiny to  tell the story there were over 47 reels  of footage miraculously it sat in hal  tolson's basement for 50 years  we had to restore it so that took five  months alone for them to just go frame  by frame by frame dusting it making sure  that it didn't snap or anything we got  that footage  it was damn near perfect  the great irony of this is that because  no one  wanted to do anything with this footage  at the time and through the years  the footage was actually in remarkably  good shape  for four to five months i lived with the  footage inside my household i constantly  kept that concert on a loop and if  something intrigued me i would take  notes during those five months i would  come across some mind-blowing footage my  jaw would drop  while there were five camera angles at  the event itself they had done their own  live  camera mix  it did not have the isolated camera  footage  i knew that we should actually approach  the editing like you do your dj gigs we  should be  just as rhythmic this film is his dj set  one of many things that amir brings to  the project as a director as a  storyteller is his perspective as a  musician often times when i curate  things there's kind of a  rhythmic feng shui thing that i  i don't know how to describe yet my job  was to basically be the turntables you  find ways to blend songs into each other  i think he understands pacing incredibly  well he was ready to be a director and  ready to tell this story in particular  he really had strong ideas about certain  tracks he wanted to start with the  stevie wonder drum solo  [Music]  i knew that i wanted the first five  minutes of the film to just gob smack  the person watching and when i saw  stevie wonder take a drum solo i was  like yo this is something that even  stevie wonder experts really haven't  seen before i just knew like  that was my first scene understanding  that that was going to be showing  audiences something that they had never  seen before i just realized the genius  of it it's just the perfect thumb print  for a mirror on this film not only was  the source material phenomenal to start  with but the sound quality of the source  material was phenomenal  [Music]  because it was a primarily black  festival for a primarily black audience  in the black epicenter of the country  there wasn't a lot of newspaper  clippings or anything else that we found  announcing the festival i think the  obvious answer is racism right like i  think let's not beat around the bush up  to that point black identity was in  question everyone dressed the same they  wore tuxedos they did uh choreograph  moves  you had to filter and make yourself  palatable so that you wouldn't scare  white people people watching in texas  people watching it in ohio and that's  where we were as a culture and it  reflected in the music and it reflected  in the message as well  so many luminaries came together from bb  king gladys knight the pips that is  [Applause]  now when i stepped on stage i was  totally taken aback because i didn't  expect a crowd like that  ladies and gentlemen  the tamers brothers  there was max roach and abby lincoln  slat music african music  sliding the family stone hugh masakayla  fifth dimension their set is incredible  it's just so catchy and colorful so many  just amazing performers  you know occasionally my jaw would drop  especially in watching that mahalia  mavis movement  [Music]  i just stood in front of my television  i'm like yo that's crazy  ladies and gentlemen  nina simone  [Music]  for her there was a burning desire to  stand up for black people and really  give a message on why they are important  why they are beautiful  [Music]  you could feel the artists drawing  energy from the audience you know i  think it was clear that the artist knew  that something magical was happening  it's just something to see  black america has lost faith in this  country 1969 was such a transformative  period somebody better wake up  day by day month by month we started  noticing the parallels of 1969 and 50  years later the same things that they  were talking about back then were  happening to us right now we want a new  life a black consciousness revolution  it reflects and brings to the forefront  a moment in time that might give us some  insight into where we are today  culturally the one takeaway that i hear  from people who had the opportunity to  see the film at a festival prior to its  opening is about how topical the film  actually is there's an easy takeaway to  see all of the parallels  about  social injustice about you know love of  community unfortunately how things  really have not evolved much in over 50  years  we need it now there are important  themes to this movie and important  things that it says about where we are  as a society we have a responsibility to  show the people the social and political  circumstances with poverty how the  government treats its citizens how the  police treated citizens the idea of  protesting and letting your voice be  heard the revolution is not going to  stop  everybody  i don't want people to get the feeling  that this is a history lesson in any way  we all put a lot of work and energy into  making sure that this is a joyful film  the the music in the film and the  performances are so exuberant it makes  you proud to be part of a world where  this could happen we'd be remiss if we  didn't  acknowledge people that attended the  festival because of the historical way  that community has been treated you  can't just get to people through social  media you have to meet them on the  ground that was really difficult it's  not as easy as it seems because 50 years  ago is a very long time in the case of  moussa jackson he told us that this was  his very first memory the things that he  described in detail were sort of like  okay he really remembers this their  outfits were orange but it was orange  like you know remember the creamsicles  like at the very end then we showed him  the footage  [Music]  he wasn't expecting that and then that's  when the tears just came rolling  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  that's when i realized like  oh this this is that type of film like  this isn't just  a cool concert like you are  you're giving people back their memories  i'm not crazy  thank you  this incredible thing did happen and  it's really important to to preserve  that memory not only for the people who  went but also for the rest of culture  at the end of the day i think a lot of  the news just came to be because us  discovering you know black is beautiful  instead of always trying to pander and  please and readjust our lives to make  certain people feel safe  let's please ourselves  [Music]  [Applause]  take the first
 

